# systemd unit for the vike-gatewayhub GATEWAY daemon — the one process that owns every connection to
# every roster venue (docs/decisions/0164-one-daemon-owns-every-venue-connection-vike-gatewayhub.md;
# the design is docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-13-vike-gatewayhub-design.md), in the
# ONE-PROJECT-FOLDER shape. See docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md for the runbook: the install order, the
# rollback and what to read in the journal.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT IS, AND WHAT IT IS TO THE OTHER UNITS. Wave 1 of that record moves every venue call the
# data daemon makes into this process: the market-data and recorder feeds, the REST of backfill and
# the chart seed, the venue catalog, the Deribit options poll and the OANDA practice-token history
# read. The data daemon (deploy/vike-datahub.service) is its one client and dials it over loopback
# through `vike-gatewayhub-client`; it keeps the store, the recorder, the md hub and every served
# verb. This daemon holds NO store, commits nothing and signs no order. The trading daemon
# (deploy/vike-tradehub.service) is UNTOUCHED in wave 1 and still opens its own venue sessions; the
# backtesthub never talks to this one.
#
# ⚠ ORDER, AND WHY IT IS `Wants=` + `After=` AND NEVER `Requires=`. The data daemon's unit orders
# itself after this one and WANTS it, so a box that starts both starts this one first. It does not
# REQUIRE it: a restart of this daemon must not take the data daemon down, whose remote clients retry
# (a recorder that stopped with it would open a gap in the tape for nothing). The converse is the
# trap this file's runbook is written around — a data daemon started with this one absent has no
# venue access at all — so install and enable THIS unit BEFORE the release that contains the data
# daemon's remote client reaches the box (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Install order").
#
# ⚠ EVERY PATH BELOW IS THE PLACEHOLDER PROJECT ROOT /srv/vike-<unit>. It is an example, not a
# requirement — the daemon works from ANY absolute path, and THE ROOT IS THE PARAMETER THIS FILE IS
# SUBSTITUTED FOR. On a box that already runs the data and trading daemons the root is THEIR project
# folder: one folder, one `settings/`, each daemon with its own write grant. Choose your own by ONE
# rule: a single directory on a disk with room, owned by the user the service runs as, holding bin/
# and settings/ (with state/ and state/logs/). Then substitute it everywhere in the installed copy and
# check nothing was missed:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   sed "s,/srv/vike-<unit>,$R,g" deploy/vike-gatewayhub.service > /tmp/vike-gatewayhub.service
#   grep -n /srv/vike-<unit> /tmp/vike-gatewayhub.service    # no output means every placeholder went
#   sudo install -Dm644 /tmp/vike-gatewayhub.service /etc/systemd/system/vike-gatewayhub.service
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now vike-gatewayhub
#
# A SYSTEM install also wants `User=`/`Group=` added and `WantedBy=` changed to `multi-user.target`
# (this file ships the `--user`-friendly `default.target`). ⚠ Read the hardening block at the bottom
# first: under a --user manager the sandbox directives are applied as NO-OPS on a stock Ubuntu box —
# measured, docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md.
#
# ⚠ ONE UNIT PER DAEMON: a daemon has ONE unit file, named without a suffix, and the project root is a
# substitutable parameter rather than a second file. WHAT A BOX SUBSTITUTES AND ADDS, as for every unit
# in this directory: the root; `User=`/`Group=`, deliberately NOT shipped here because this file is
# published to the public source mirror, whose redaction rewrites a box's user name into a phrase that
# is not a legal systemd user name; `WantedBy=multi-user.target` for a SYSTEM install; and, where the
# box wants a failure pager, `OnFailure=vike-notify@%n.service` inline OR deploy/attach-onfailure.sh's
# drop-in — never both, `OnFailure=` is a LIST directive and the notifier would fire twice.
#
# ⚠ THE INSTALLED COPY OF A SIBLING UNIT IS A HAND-DERIVED ARTIFACT, NOT A RENDERING OF ITS TEMPLATE.
# MEASURED on the live box 2026-10-13: the installed data unit names no `--store` argument (the store
# is its `config.store_root` row) and grants the box's `data` directory where the tracked template
# grants `market_data`. Do not diff this file against a sibling's installed copy and read the
# difference as a defect; a unit on that box is installed by hand from the tracked file and drifts only
# by hand (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "The installed units differ from their templates").
#
# The shape is MACHINE-CHECKED: crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/deploy_layout_gate.rs reads every
# unit in this directory and fails if one names a second location, spreads its write grant over more
# than one ReadWritePaths= LINE, grants a path that covers a binary it runs or its own settings
# directory, sets VIKE_LOG_DIR, points EnvironmentFile= at the credential store, or runs an
# ExecStartPre= binary from outside the project folder (or behind a `-`, which would make the
# pre-check non-fatal).

[Unit]
Description=vike-gatewayhub — gateway daemon: the one process that owns the venue connections the data daemon reads through (wave 1 serves market data, history, catalog and options)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple

# ⚠ **THE RELEASE SHIPS ONE BACKEND BINARY, `vike-backend`, AND `gatewayhub` IS A TOOL INSIDE IT** —
# the same multicall deploy/vike-datahub.service runs as `datahub`; the two units differ only in the
# verb. Dispatch is on a SUBCOMMAND, so there is no symlink here and no `203/EXEC` class to inherit
# from a dangling one. The binary and the verb are ONE edit, never two: `crates/vike/src/lib.rs`'s
# `resolve` matches a tool by EXACT string with no aliases, so `bin/vike-backend vike-gatewayhub` is
# exit 2 with a tool list — a daemon that "started" and did nothing, not a crash.
# The standalone crate binary (`vike-gatewayhub`) exists too and is what the crate's own tests spawn;
# nothing on a box installs it. Building the shipped one yourself:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-backend --bin vike-backend --features full
#
# ⚠ THE BINARY AND THIS FILE REACH THE BOX SEPARATELY, AND NOTHING SEQUENCES THEM.
# `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy` replaces the BINARY, unattended, on a tag; THIS file only reaches
# the box when an operator runs the `install -Dm644` recipe above and `systemctl daemon-reload`, and
# that helper restarts this unit only if the INSTALLED copy of the helper itself names it (it is
# installed by hand too: docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Install order", step 1). The daemon takes NO
# flags: everything it can be told is a settings row, and a typo on this line is refused with exit 2
# rather than ignored.
#
# WorkingDirectory IS the project root: the settings walk and the rolling log resolve off it with no
# extra variable (crates/vike-paths/src/state_path.rs's `project_state_dir`).
WorkingDirectory=/srv/vike-<unit>
ExecStart=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend gatewayhub

# --- PRE-FLIGHT: refuse a broken configuration instead of starting degraded ----------------------
# The same check deploy/vike-datahub.service and deploy/vike-tradehub.service run, for the same
# reason: `vike-cli config check` resolves the SAME settings directory through the SAME loader this
# daemon uses and makes the EXIT CODE the product, so a non-zero status fails the unit BEFORE
# ExecStart runs. docs/decisions/0013-degrade-vs-refuse.md is the rule it implements. The daemon's
# own boot (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/gatewayhub_cli/boot.rs`'s `boot_root`) loads settings too, so
# on a box with this line a broken tree is met here, once, rather than as a restart loop.
#
# ⚠ Deliberately NOT `--strict` (it promotes a 0644 credential-store permission finding into a
# refusal the workspace argues must never be one), and NO leading `-` (that would make a failed
# pre-check non-fatal, i.e. delete this line while appearing to have it).
# ⚠ It needs a second binary in bin/, or systemd fails the unit with status=203/EXEC:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-cli --bin vike-cli
#   install -m755 target/release/vike-cli /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli
ExecStartPre=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli config check

# --- the listen address --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The listener binds the `config.gatewayhub_addr` row of this box's settings database, and
# loopback port 7883 when the row is absent — which is what this template relies on. A non-loopback
# address is REFUSED at startup with exit 2, and there is NO flag, no key and no row that lifts it:
# the daemon holds every venue connection and its handshake is plaintext, so it serves this host only
# (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/gatewayhub_cli/boot.rs`'s `resolve_bind` is the rule). Reach it from
# elsewhere through an SSH tunnel to its loopback port. Do not restate the bind as a property in
# `Description=` — see
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/deploy_layout_gate/descriptions_and_hardening.rs`'s
# `no_unit_description_claims_what_an_environment_file_can_falsify`.
# ⚠ The row is also what the DATA DAEMON dials. Move one and the other with it, or every venue call
# fails at its first connect, naming the address it tried.
#
# ⚠ **NO KEYS, NO START.** This daemon holds venue credentials, so unlike a key-less data daemon it
# has no unauthenticated mode: with no gateway hub key in the credential store it logs why and exits.
# Mint the keys BEFORE enabling this unit (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Install order", step 2).

# --- WHERE SETTINGS AND CREDENTIALS LIVE ---------------------------------------------------------
# `<project>/settings/` holds the settings database and `state/`. No settings file is read (decision
# 0086) and no setting is read from the process environment (decision 0111): a retired variable in
# this unit REFUSES startup, naming the row that replaced it (`vike_config::REMOVED_ENV`). The binary
# finds the project by walking UP from its working directory; the line below makes the answer
# EXPLICIT and independent of WorkingDirectory — set it and no walk happens at all.
#
# ⚠ A DEPLOYED box has a BINARY, not a source checkout, so the directory must exist, AND its `state/`
# sub-directory (and `state/logs/`), which under ProtectSystem=strict the daemon cannot create for
# itself:
#   install -d -m700 /srv/vike-<unit>/settings /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs
#
# ⚠⚠ **ONE DAEMON READS THE VENUE CREDENTIALS, AND THIS IS IT** (decision 0164, verdict 6). It reads
# them by SCOPED reads after boot — its hub keys, and in wave 1 one OANDA practice-token field of
# one account, once per history request — never the whole venue map at boot, and a value reaches no
# log line, no reply and no `Debug`. The venue settings rows (`venue.<name>.*`) are READS too.
Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings

# ⚠ NO `EnvironmentFile=`, and that is the point rather than an omission. The directive is a second
# lever on the process environment that WINS over every `Environment=` line here (measured, both
# orders), and on a shared root it reaches the OTHER daemons' operator file: one stray line there
# would arrive in the one process that holds every venue key. Everything this daemon is told is a
# settings ROW; the console's `RUST_LOG` is the one developer switch and is not a unit concern.

# --- turn the trace FILE log DOWN ----------------------------------------------------------------
# RUST_LOG and the log_level row filter the CONSOLE only; the file layer defaults to `trace`. The file
# level is the `preferences.log_file_level` ROW (no line in this file sets it); write it once, as the
# service user with this unit's VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR, before the first start:
#   vike-cli config set preferences.log_file_level warn
# ⚠ Deliberately NO VIKE_LOG_DIR. Unset, the project layer resolves the rolling log to
# <project>/settings/state/logs — the one path of the write grant at the bottom of this file, shared
# with the data daemon's log directory under a prefix of its own (`vike-gatewayhub`), so the two do not
# prune each other's files. Watch it with `du -sh /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs`.

# --- keep the daemon from starving the box -------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE CAP IS A SUM OF FOUR TERMS, and the unit's life is longer than wave 1: this daemon holds every
# fetch ANSWER whole (a lane returns the full `Vec` of rows and the daemon pages it out of memory), and
# it is the process wave 2 puts the Dukascopy JVM and the FXCM native SDK in. A child process and a
# loaded library both count against THIS cgroup.
#
#   steady  MEASURED on the live box 2026-10-13 (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md carries the table): the
#           data daemon's client-owning half is 45 threads, 6 established venue sockets and a resident
#           set of about 0.5 GB, 1.0 GB at its high-water. That is the WHOLE data daemon, store and
#           page cache included, so it over-states the half that moved here; the stream queues add at
#           most their ceilings (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/limits.rs`'s
#           `STREAM_PLANE_MEMORY_CEILING_BYTES`, 128 MiB, and the options feeds' 8 x 4 MiB).
#           1.0 GB + 0.16 GB = about 1.2 GB.
#   jvm     WAVE 2, and NOT MEASURED: wave 1 starts no JVM.
#           `crates/bridges/dukascopy/src/exec.rs`'s `jvm_args` launches `java -Duser.home=... -jar
#           <bridge jar>` and emits NO `-Xmx` and no `-Xms`, so the heap is the JVM's default: a quarter
#           of the memory the JVM believes it has. A JDK 17 that finds this unit's cgroup limit sizes it
#           from MemoryMax (a quarter of 8G is 2 GB of heap); one that does not (a `--user` manager, a
#           hidden /sys/fs/cgroup) sizes it from the box (a quarter of 125 GB), which no MemoryMax could
#           hold. The launch SHOULD carry an explicit `-Xmx` (512m as a start value, and the heap is only
#           half the process: metaspace, the code cache, thread stacks and the GC's tables add a few
#           hundred MB). Planned: 0.5 GB heap + 0.5 GB the rest = 1.0 GB for the ONE sidecar. Until the
#           flag exists the term is 2 GB under this cap and unbounded outside it. Measure with the sidecar
#           under this unit (`systemd-cgtop`, or `VmHWM` of the java pid) before trusting it.
#   fxcm    WAVE 2. The ForexConnect SDK (`libForexConnect.so` behind `libfcshim.so`) lives IN the
#           process (decision 0164, "Measured on the live box"), in native memory the Rust allocator
#           never sees. Its footprint is not measured separately; the one bound there is: the trading
#           daemon hosts it today (its FXCM demo account is active and armed) inside cgroup peaks of 88
#           to 205 MB per run for ALL its venue sessions (`t0_baselines.md` section 5). 0.25 GB.
#   answer  ONE kline request is ONE batch: `backfill_kline_source` asks the source once for the whole
#           window the caller names, and nothing in the datahub bounds that window, so the largest
#           answer is the largest window an operator asks for. A `Bar` is 120 B, a funding point 32 B,
#           a `QuoteTick` 72 B plus its symbol (`crates/vike-gatewayhub-client/src/proto_tests.rs`'s
#           `the_row_sizes_the_unit_memory_arithmetic_uses` measures them). Ten million bars (19 years of
#           1m, or about four months of 1s) is 1.2 GB, held ONCE: the pager makes one page at a time
#           (it used to build a second copy of the whole answer, so the peak was twice that; measured on
#           the lane, 3 million bars: 345 MB of rows, +343 MB to split them eagerly, +3 MB lazily). A tick
#           window is capped at 31 days (`GW_TICKS_MAX_SPAN_MS`); its quote count is not measured here, and
#           at several million quotes of about 100 B each it is the same order. The chunked lanes (a day of
#           ticks, a day of OANDA bars) are far under it.
#
#   1.2 GB steady + 1.0 GB jvm + 0.25 GB fxcm = 2.45 GB that is there whatever is being fetched; 8G leaves
#   5.5 GB, which is FOUR answers of 1.2 GB (7.25 GB in all). 8G is the cap of the data daemon's unit
#   (deploy/vike-datahub.service), where these same buffers lived until the venue calls moved here, so a
#   request that fitted there fits here. Wave 1 alone (no JVM, no FXCM session) holds five. It is a
#   BACKSTOP and not a promise: `MAX_FETCHES` (32) fetches at the worst window would not fit.
#
# ⚠ WHAT A BREACH DOES. A MemoryMax breach is an OOM kill inside THIS unit's cgroup, and it takes the
# whole unit down: the kernel picks a victim by badness inside the cgroup (`OOMScoreAdjust=` only
# reorders the candidates; it cannot exempt the daemon, and it would make the JVM child the likelier
# victim), and systemd's default `OOMPolicy=stop` then stops the rest. Every stream session ends, every
# client reconnects after `Restart=` below, and a half-open JForex login is lost with it. So the cap is
# where a request too large for the box ends, not a way to spare the other sessions.
# The old 2G was sized from the steady set alone. Tune to the box's headroom (125 GB on the CI box).
MemoryMax=8G
# Restart on a crash (an OOM kill included), but not on a clean exit / operator stop.
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

# --- stop ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `systemctl stop` IS a graceful stop. SIGTERM raises the same stop flag the daemon's whole run order
# is built around (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/gatewayhub_cli.rs`'s `run_with` arms the handlers
# before anything can block) and the accept loop drains: every connection is told, a stream session
# writes its `Bye`, a running fetch is cancelled and the connection threads are joined. The decision,
# the measurements and the options that were refused are in docs/ops/graceful-stop.md.
# There is deliberately NO ExecStop=: the daemon stops on the signal, so a stop command could only add
# a SECOND stop route to a design whose whole invariant is that there is one.
KillSignal=SIGTERM
# ⚠ THE BUDGET IS DERIVED, and `crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/graceful_stop_pin.rs`'s
# `the_gatewayhub_unit_outlasts_its_stop_drain` reads it out of THIS file. The one bounded wait in the
# teardown is `crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/accept.rs`'s `serve`, which joins the connection
# threads for at most `crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/limits.rs`'s `STOP_DRAIN` (half of it politely,
# then the sockets are cut) and returns whatever has not finished: a thread still inside a venue call
# is left behind and ends with the process. Nothing here is buffered — the daemon holds no store and
# commits nothing, the data daemon owns every row — so a SIGKILL costs the clients a reconnect and
# no data. So the number is the drain, plus that gate's fixed allowance for what runs after `serve`
# returns (the lane drop and the exit), plus the same allowance again as margin. Raise the drain and
# raise this too; if the wire hook ever JOINS something after `serve`, that wait joins this sum.
TimeoutStopSec=15

# --- the sandbox ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The daemon needs no privileges, makes outbound connections to the venues and one loopback listener.
NoNewPrivileges=true
# ⚠ ProtectSystem=strict mounts the WHOLE filesystem read-only except /dev, /proc, /sys and whatever
# ReadWritePaths= names. READS are unaffected, so the settings and the credential store load normally.
#
# ⚠ MEASURED on the live box (systemd 255, Ubuntu 24.04): under a `systemd --user` manager with the
# distribution default `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`, these directives are applied
# as NO-OPS — no warning, no failed unit, and `systemctl --user show` still reports them as
# configured. They enforce under the SYSTEM manager. docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md has the probe.
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=true
# ONE grant, ONE path, and deliberately the SUB-directory: this daemon writes its rolling log and
# NOTHING else in wave 1. The OANDA token and the venue settings are READS, it commits no store row,
# it checks no HALT sentinel (it places no order) and it persists no rotated credential — so none of
# `settings/state` (the trading daemon's HALT, journal and lock files), the settings database or the
# tape is granted, and a daemon that cannot write its own configuration has none to lose.
#
# ⚠ **THE GRANT GROWS HERE, IN THIS FILE, NEVER IN A DROP-IN.** A later wave that makes this daemon
# persist a rotated cTrader pair or claim a live-account lock adds its path to THIS one line, with
# the argument, in the same change as the code that needs it. A grant that exists only in an
# `/etc/systemd/system/*.d/` file is a grant no review of this repository ever saw. The path must
# EXIST (the install recipe above creates it): under the SYSTEM manager systemd refuses a unit whose
# `ReadWritePaths=` names a missing path (status=226), and that refusal is the point.
# ⚠ ONE LINE if it ever names several paths: deploy_layout_gate.rs fails a unit that spreads its grant
# over more than one ReadWritePaths= line, because a second line is where a grant nobody re-read gets
# added. NOT the project root either: that would cover bin/ (the binary this unit executes) and
# settings/ (the credential store), which the same gate refuses.
ReadWritePaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs


# --- syscall / capability hardening, MEASURED ON THE REAL BINARIES -------------------------------
# The tradehub's whole block, VERBATIM, except one directive. MEASURED on the CI box 2026-08-17, systemd
# 255, with throwaway SYSTEM units running these exact binaries — every line survived a run, the TLS
# handshakes and WebSocket dials included. The set is the one every daemon unit in this directory
# carries; `crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/deploy_layout_gate/descriptions_and_hardening.rs`'s
# `REQUIRED_HARDENING` is the minimum and its `every_daemon_unit_keeps_its_syscall_and_capability_hardening`
# the rule.
#
# ⚠⚠ **`MemoryDenyWriteExecute=` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND IT IS THE ONLY DIRECTIVE THIS UNIT OMITS.**
# MEASURED on the live box 2026-10-13 (decision 0164, "Measured on the live box"): the JForex JRE
# (Temurin 17.0.19) REFUSES TO START under `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes`, printing
# "Failed to mark memory page as executable", under the JIT and `-Xint` alike, and starts with it off
# and the rest of the sandbox unchanged. deploy/vike-ibkr-gateway.service already runs without it for
# the same JIT reason (its header says so). This daemon is where the Dukascopy JVM sidecar will be
# spawned (wave 2), so the sandbox of the process that owns it cannot carry the directive.
# ⚠ Wave 1 STARTS NO JVM — its Dukascopy fetch is the keyless `.bi5` archive read — so the directive
# could stay ON until wave 2 and cost nothing; the owner chose to omit it now (record 0164, verdict
# 7(c)), so the unit does not have to change twice. ⚠ NOT a loophole to copy: every other daemon unit
# keeps it, `deploy_layout_gate`'s `every_daemon_unit_keeps_memory_deny_write_execute_unless_declared`
# holds that and names this unit as the one declared exemption, with this reason. The record's
# reopener: Dukascopy reachable without a JVM, or a need to restore the directive — the JVM then
# gets its own unit.
#
# ⚠ PrivateNetwork= is deliberately ABSENT and always will be — this daemon IS a network client.
# `systemd-analyze` charges for that and for holding AF_INET sockets at all; both are unrecoverable
# for anything that talks to a venue. RestrictAddressFamilies= therefore ALLOWS the three families in
# use rather than pretending the internet is optional.
#
# ⚠ The one line with a real blast radius is UMask=0077: new files become owner-only. A future reader
# under a DIFFERENT account breaks on it, loudly, with a permission error rather than silence. That
# is the intended trade for a tree holding venue keys.
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
LockPersonality=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallFilter=~@privileged @resources @obsolete @mount @module @reboot @swap @raw-io @debug @clock @cpu-emulation
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
UMask=0077

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
